2 CONT SOURCE CITATION:
2 CONT    Page: 198
2 CONT    Title: Cavaliers and Pioneers.
2 CONT    Author: Niel Marion Nugent, com,p.
2 CONT    Publication Information: Rpt., Baltimore MD, Genealogical.
2 CONT    Publishing Co.,Inc., 1974.
2 CONT SOURCE CITATION:
2 CONT    Page: 1
2 CONT    Title: Descendants of James Hardaway, Charles City County,
2 CONT    Virginia,1650-1800
2 CONT    Author: James F. Devenport, Judith Hardaway Devenport, Laurence
2 CONT    B. Gardiner
2 CONT    Publication Information: James F. Devenport, One Winchester
2 CONT    Court, Midland,Texas 79705, 1992
2 CONT 
2 CONT 
2 CONT 
2 CONT 
2 CONT SOURCE CITATION:
2 CONT    Page: page xxvi
2 CONT    Text: Meyer,Dorman:  The term headright has no significance other
2 CONT    than as evidence of the right to own land.  In order to stimulate
2 CONT    settlement, The Virginia Company made the provision that every
2 CONT    person who, after 1616 came into the Colony from overseas or
2 CONT    from a neighboring settlement, was entitled to 50 acres of land in
2 CONT    his own name or in the name of the person who paid the passage.
2 CONT    Devenport:  The person named as headright in a patent did not
2 CONT    necessarily arrive in the colony the year the patent was
2 CONT    issued but such date oftentimes is the only clue to the person's  first
2 CONT    appearance in Virginia.  This is the case with John Hardaway and
2 CONT    later his brother James Hardaway.
2 CONT    Title: Adventures of Purse and Person, Virginia 1607-1624/5,
2 CONT    Author: Virginia M. Meyer and John Frederick Dorman, eds.
2 CONT    Publication Information: 3rd ed, Richmond Va: The Dietz Press,
2 CONT    Inc., 1987
2 CONT SOURCE CITATION:
2 CONT    Page: page 204
2 CONT    Text: Tradition has it that the Hardaways, John and James
2 CONT    emigrated to the Virginia Colonies from Blandford, England, and
2 CONT    established themselves into the English society along the southside
2 CONT    of the James River in Charles City County.  One can feel certain both
2 CONT    men were proud Englishmen, loyal to both God and King, and willing
2 CONT    to endure hardships of the colonies so that their children and
2 CONT    their children's children might enjoy a better life.
2 CONT    Fleet:  Just when and where John and James Hardaway were born is
2 CONT       unknown.  Whether they were married before leaving England, or
2 CONT       after arrival in the Virginia Colonies, and to whom they married,
2 CONT       has been lost to time.  The early Virginia records reveal precious            little in
2 CONC formation about these men, but James Hardaway by 1658 had
2 CONT       been nominated and elected Constable for Westover Precinct.
2 CONT       John Hardaway appears to have died after 3 Oct 1660 and before
2 CONT       3 Dec 1660, as there appears an entry  dated 3 Oct 1660 in the
2 CONT       Charles City records stating thet John Jones, servant to John
2 CONT       Hardaway, had his time doubled.  Fleet, p 234.  This is followed              shortly t
2 CONC hereafter by several entries where the estate of John
2 CONT       Hardaway, deceased, was ordered to pay certain individulas varying
2 CONT       amounts oftobacco.  Fleet p 237 four entries.
2 CONT       John Hardaway apparently left a will, as can be seen from
2 CONT       the Court Order dated 3 October 1662, which reads in part:
2 CONT         The co'rt confirmeth an order formerly granted by mr. Anthony
2 CONT       Syatt to Morgan Jones for Rob't Baker for three hundred pounds
2 CONT       of tobacko and Caske with costs against James Hardaway the ieire
2 CONT       and Execut'r of Jo:  Hardaway dec'd and granteth exec on non paym't
2 CONT       thereof.  Fleet, p 265.
2 CONT       Perhaps the strongest documented evidence to survive of the
2 CONT       relationship between John and james Hardaway can be found in
2 CONT       that certain Deed of Gift, dated 5 September 1664 and recorded in
2 CONT       Charles City County Court Records, in whis is stated,
2 CONT       Be itknowne unto all men by these presents that whereas the
2 CONT       inheritence of 250 acres of land scituated {situated} on the
2 CONT       upper side of Kemishes Creeke descended and divolved {devised}
2 CONT       to me James Hardway by the decesse [decease] of my late brother
2 CONT       John Hardway.
2 CONT       Werefor the said James Hardway xx give[s] the said 250 acres xx
2 CONT       unto by sonn John Hardway and his heirs forever xx
2 CONT       His mark S  Witnessed by Charles Power and Robert Rowse.
2 CONT    Title: Virginia Colonial Abstracts, vol. III
2 CONT    Author: Beverly Fleet
2 CONT    Publication Information: Balitmore, MD: Genealogical Publishing
2 CONT Co., Inc.,1988
2 CONT Devenport- It is said that the first Hardaway of Colonial Virginia
2 CONT was John Hardaway who emigrated from England prior to 1650.  Whether
2 CONT lured by opportunity or driven by necessity, John Hardaway, like
2 CONT hundreds of other determined individuals, made his way to America
2 CONT and settled in Charles City County, Vriginia.
2 CONT    A patent dated 12 August 1650 in Patent Book No. 2, page 248,
2 CONT shows Richard Tye and Charles Sparrowe entered a patent for 2500
2 CONT acres of land in Charles City County, near the head of Powells Creek
2 CONT and adjacent to land called Weyanoke Old Town, claiming as one of
2 CONT ther headrights, JON. HARDAWAY for whom they had furnished
2 CONT transportation. (Nugent)
