1 NOTE SOURCE CITATION:
2 CONT    Page: page 239
2 CONT    Title: Virginia Colonial Abstracts, vol. III
2 CONT    Author: Beverly Fleet
2 CONT    Publication Information: Balitmore, MD: Genealogical Publishing
2 CONT Co., Inc.,
2 CONT 1988
2 CONT Fleet:  On or about 2 February 1660/s Thomas Rowe [Rowse?] was
2 CONT ordered to give bond for the estate of Jo. Hardway [deceased] and
2 CONT the care of orphan.  This suggests that John Hardaway had at least
2 CONT one minor heir, concievably a minor female.  There is no mention of
2 CONT John's wife leading to the conclusion she preceded him in death.
2 CONT This fact just is not known.
2 CONT      Another clue into the expanding Hardaway webb can be found in
2 CONT Charles City Court Order dated 18 October 1664, ordering Thomas Roe
2 CONT [Rowse?] to provide another man to be bound w him to secure estate
2 CONT and education of jOhn Hardaway's orphan (Meyer and Dorman p xxv)
2 CONT Here again the singular or the word orphan is used, thereby
2 CONT reinforcing the implicaton of an heir.  According to English law,
2 CONT which the colonials adopted, landed estate was held in fee tail and
2 CONT automatically passed from father to son or to the nearest male heir.
2 CONT  The borther, James Hardaway's inheritance of the realestate
2 CONT strengthens the hypothesis of a female heir and that he, James
2 CONT Hardaway, is the nearest male relative.  Regarding the orphan, who
2 CONT she was or what may have become of her is not revealed from  the
2 CONT records.
2 CONT The first indication that John Hardaway owned property in Charles
2 CONT City County appears in a deed, dated 22 April, 1661, from Walter
2 CONT Alston of Charles City to William Edwards of a parcel of land
2 CONT adjacent to that of Willliam Edqards and John Hardway on Kemishes
2 CONT Creek (Fleet p 240).  Whether John Hardaway had been issued a patent
2 CONT and failed to file the patent with the court is not known.
